---
/*
  /listening — the sets: curated listens, one Spotify playlist to a sentence
  (plan 40 §3, ADR 0035). The room is `MusicSets.astro`; this file is only the
  address, the read, and the empty state.

  ⚠ THE ROOM IS NAMED FOR THE ACT AND NOT FOR THE ARTIFACT, AND THAT IS THE
  SECOND HALF OF ADR 0040. It shipped as `/music` for one afternoon and Michael
  caught what the word was doing: *"it implies too much that I may be a musician
  and that I'm trying to share music I made, but may lead the user to be
  disappointed that I'm merely sharing playlists."* On a personal site, beside
  About, `Music` is the discography slot — so the label was making a claim the
  site cannot back, which is a credibility cost before it is ever a
  disappointment. **`Listening` cannot be misread as authorship**: it names what
  Michael does with this music rather than what he made of it, and it is the
  name `design.md` §13 has carried as an open question — *"whether `/listening`
  exists"* — since July, never struck through until now.

  ⚠ AND THE SCHEMA DID NOT FOLLOW, deliberately: `sets`, `songs`, `MusicSets`,
  `scripts/music-sets.ts` and `/admin/sets` all keep their names. Those describe
  what the corpus HOLDS; this describes what a reader is invited to do. Same
  division `index.astro` argues for the word "jazz" — the copy a stranger reads
  is not a column name.

  ⚠ IT WAS `/blog?view=music` UNTIL 2026-08-21, AND THE MOVE REVERSES A RULING
  (ADR 0040). Plan 33 §4 ruled the other way — *"Music is a third register"*, a
  view of the Index rather than a room — and the code went on agreeing with it
  right up to this commit: no rail, no feed, no search, no pagination, a
  different vocabulary, and `view !== 'music'` guarding the two text views'
  entire machinery. That is the case for a ROOM, made in the negative for four
  months. What settled it was a question about chrome rather than about music:
  Michael wanted a stranger to see the site collects more than writing, and the
  nav could only say so by drawing three chrome links to three states of one
  page — directly over the page's own switch, which is the thing ADR 0030 had
  just finished making the only masthead. A view state is not a destination. So
  the register became a route and the nav got a fourth room instead of a fork.

  ⚠ NOTHING HERE DRAWS A VISIBLE HEADING, which is `/about`'s pattern and
  `MusicSets`' own rule (see its header): the index of set titles, set in
  display type, IS this page's masthead. A "Music" printed above it would be
  the double print that killed the `stacked` arrangement on the `/lab/sets`
  bench — Michael's sentence, then Spotify's copy of it, then ours. The h1 is
  `sr-only` for the document outline and for nothing else.

  ⚠ THE TAB'S "WAIT UNTIL THERE IS SOMETHING BEHIND IT" GATE RETIRES WITH THE
  TAB, and deliberately rather than by omission. It was a gate on ANNOUNCING —
  the route always worked, only the switch waited on one published set — and
  the announcement has now been made in the top bar of every page on the site.
  Re-gating it there would mean a count query in `SiteLayout` on EVERY route,
  public and admin, to answer a question whose answer is yes and stays yes. The
  empty state below is what covers the case instead, which is what it was
  always for.
*/
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import MusicSets from '../components/MusicSets.astro';
import { resolveSet } from '../lib/music-sets';
import { listSets } from '../lib/sets';

const supabase = Astro.locals.supabase;
const sets = await listSets(supabase);
// `?set=<slug>` — ONE set open, never a combination. A stale link falls back to
// the first rather than to an empty pane; see `resolveSet`.
const selectedSet = resolveSet(Astro.url.searchParams.get('set'), sets);

// The same 60s / SWR-a-day pair the other four public rooms answer with. No
// `Vary` here, unlike `/blog`: nothing on this page fetches a partial, so there
// is no second variant for the edge to hold.
Astro.response.headers.set('Cache-Control', 'public, s-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate=86400');
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Listening"
  active="listening"
  description="What Michael has been listening to — a jazz playlist to a sentence, with the words and the quote that go with it."
>
  {
    /* ⚠ MORE AIR THAN `/blog` CARRIES, AND THAT IS THE TAB'S SPACE REDISTRIBUTED
      RATHER THAN A NEW OPINION. On the Index this room sat under a `text-3xl`
      switch with `mb-8` beneath it — about 7rem of page above the first
      sentence. Landing the index directly under a 3.25rem running head instead
      starts the room abruptly against the chrome, and these lines are display
      type, which wants the margin a title would have taken. Half of what the
      switch occupied, kept. */
  }
  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl px-5 py-10 sm:py-14">
    <h1 class="sr-only">Listening</h1>
    {sets.length > 0 && <MusicSets sets={sets} selected={selectedSet} />}
    {
      /* Michael sees this while filling the room; a stranger following a link
        into an emptied one sees it once. Same register as the room's own. */
      sets.length === 0 && (
        <div class="flex min-h-[16rem] flex-col items-center justify-center px-6 py-16 text-center">
          <p class="text-whisper max-w-sm font-serif text-lg">Nothing here yet.</p>
          <p class="text-whisper mt-2 max-w-sm font-sans text-sm">
            The playlists collect here one at a time, as they are made.
          </p>
        </div>
      )
    }
  </div>
</SiteLayout>

<script>
  // The sets take ownership of a pane the server already rendered correctly.
  // `astro:page-load` covers every arrival AFTER the first — see the script's
  // own note on why that listener lives in the module rather than here.
  import { wireMusicSets } from '../scripts/music-sets';
  wireMusicSets();
</script>
